This is the kind of challenge that separates good code from great architecture. 

 When you have four distinct themes (Flat Dark, White Clay, Neo Brutalism, and Minimalism), you cannot hardcode colors or borders inside your JavaScript. If you do, the app will break or flash incorrect colors when you switch themes. 

 To solve this, I have refactored the entire application into a State-Driven CSS Architecture . 

 The JS is now "Blind": The JavaScript no longer knows what color the progress bar or the buttons are. It simply adds structural classes like .is-complete or .opt-badge . 

 The CSS acts as the "Brain": All visual logic is handled purely by switching the data-theme attribute on the <body> tag. 

 The Header Dropdown: I replaced the pill slider with a sleek, native Bootstrap dropdown attached to a <i class="fa-solid fa-swatchbook"></i> icon in the header. It handles switching between all 4 themes cleanly on mobile. 

