Of course. That's a fantastic feature and the next logical step for building a robust agentic framework. Instead of assembling teams on the fly, you want a dedicated interface to formally create, define, and save a multi-agent crew, including its manager and specialists. 

 I've implemented this new feature for you. It involves three parts: 

 Updating the Database ( models.py ) to store the concept of a "Crew". 

 Creating a new User Interface ( crew_management.html ) for building your crews. 

 Adding the required Backend Logic ( web_interface.py ) to handle the new UI and save the crews to the database. 

 Here are the complete, ready-to-use files. 

 ### 1. Database Update ( db/models.py ) 

 First, we need to teach our database what a "Crew" is. Add these two new classes to your models.py file. This creates a new crews table and an association table to link agents to crews. 

 Add this code to db/models.py : 

 # Add these imports at the top of your models.py
from sqlalchemy.orm import relationship

# ... keep all your existing models like Session, Transaction, Agent, etc. ...

class Crew(Base):
 __tablename__ = "crews"
 id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)
 name = Column(String, unique=True, index=True)
 description = Column(Text)
 # This relationship links a Crew to its members through the association table
 members = relationship("CrewAgent", back_populates="crew")

class CrewAgent(Base):
 __tablename__ = "crew_agents"
 crew_id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), ForeignKey("crews.id"), primary_key=True)
 agent_id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), ForeignKey("agents.id"), primary_key=True)
 role = Column(String, default='specialist') # Can be 'manager' or 'specialist'

 # Relationships to easily access the Crew and Agent objects
 crew = relationship("Crew", back_populates="members")
 agent = relationship("Agent", back_populates="crew_associations")

# Also, add the corresponding back-reference to your existing Agent class
class Agent(Base):
 __tablename__ = "agents"
 id = Column(UUID(as_uuid=True), primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)
 name = Column(String)
 type = Column(String)
 description = Column(String)
 llm_used = Column(String)
 prompt_template = Column(String)
 config = Column(JSON)
 output_format = Column(String, default="")
 is_active = Column(Boolean, default=True)

 # Add this line to the Agent model
 crew_associations = relationship("CrewAgent", back_populates="agent")

