 Then open your browser to http://127.0.0.1:5001/apidocs/ . 

 How to Use: 

 Prepare Service X: Deploy the modified Service X (with the /debug/memory_stats endpoint) to your staging server. Ensure tracemalloc is started and the endpoint is accessible from your local machine's IP. Note down the URL (e.g., http://<staging-server-ip>:<port>/debug/memory_stats ). 

 Run Monitoring Service: Start the memory_profiler.py script on your local machine. 

 Access Swagger UI: Open http://127.0.0.1:5001/apidocs/ in your browser. 

 Start Load Test (using Locust): Get your Locust test ready to run against the staging Service X. 

 Take Initial Snapshot: 

 Go to the /take_snapshot endpoint in Swagger UI. 

 Enter the target_url of Service X's debug endpoint. 

 Enter a snapshot_label like before_load_test . 

 (Optional) Enter the secret_key if you configured one. 

 Execute the request. Verify a .json file appears in the snapshots directory. 

 Run Load Test: Start your Locust load test against Service X. 

 Take Interval Snapshots: At desired intervals (e.g., 1 hour, 3 hours, 1 day), use the /take_snapshot endpoint again with appropriate labels (e.g., 1hr_mark , 3hr_mark , 1day_mark ). 

 Take Final Snapshot: After the load test completes, take a final snapshot (e.g., after_load_test ). 

 List Snapshots: Use the /list_snapshots endpoint to see all the saved snapshot files. 

 Compare Snapshots: 

 Use the /compare endpoint. 

 Provide the filenames of two snapshots you want to compare (e.g., snapshot_before_load_test_....json and snapshot_after_load_test_....json ). 

 Execute and examine the JSON response showing the deltas in OS, process, and Python memory metrics, including changes in the top allocating lines. Look for significant increases in RSS/VMS and total_allocated_by_top_n_kb . Investigate the "new" or "changed" allocations with large positive deltas. 

 Visualize Trends: 

 Use the /visualize_leak endpoint. 

 Provide a comma-separated list of snapshot filenames in chronological order (e.g., snapshot_before_load_test_....json,snapshot_1hr_mark_....json,snapshot_final_....json ). 

 Execute the request. A PNG image showing memory trends over time will be returned. Look for upward trends in Process RSS/VMS and Python allocations, potentially correlating with a downward trend in OS Available Memory. 

 This setup provides a robust way to capture memory snapshots remotely during a load test and analyze them locally using both textual comparisons and visualizations, helping pinpoint potential memory leaks or high usage areas at both the OS and Python code levels (specifically allocation sites).

