Of course. The issue is that the first pkill command is likely terminating the make process itself, so the second pkill command for the agentic process is never executed. 

 The make: *** ... Terminated message is the key clue. It means make received a signal to shut down, which can happen if pkill -f finds a match in its own parent process line. 

 A simple and robust way to fix this is to modify the search pattern slightly so it can't match itself. 

 The Corrected stop Target 

 You can make the pkill command more precise by using a common trick: placing the first letter of the process name in square brackets. This prevents the pkill command's own process from matching the pattern it's searching for. 

 Here is the corrected stop rule. Notice the [u] in the first pkill and [p] in the second. 

 stop: ## Stop all running processes
 @echo "🛑 Stopping processes..."
 pkill -f "[u]v run mcp_server_refactored.py" || true
 pkill -f "[p]oetry run uvicorn api.main:app --reload" || true
 @echo "✅ All processes stopped"

 This change will ensure both commands are found and stopped correctly without terminating the make process itself. 

