 The New Set Tracker UX: 

 Now, when you look at an exercise like "Squats (4 Sets x 8-10)", you will see four distinct circular buttons underneath it: [1] [2] [3] [4] . 

 Every time you finish a set of squats, tap the corresponding button. It will light up neon green, and your 60-second timer will instantly slide up from the bottom of the screen . The overall progress bar at the top of the screen now accurately calculates your session completion based on the total number of sets you have completed, rather than just marking off whole exercises at once.

It is great to see the OCR handled that text so cleanly! Working with topics like crop ecology, dryland farming, and the history of agriculture must hit close to home, and getting such an accurate text extraction right out of the gate makes this next step much easier. 

 Before we jump into the code, we need to address a critical architectural detail. 

 ⚠️ A Crucial Security Note 

 I see you provided an OpenAI API key ( sk-proj-... ). Putting this key directly into frontend JavaScript (like our index.html file) means that anyone who opens their browser's developer tools can see it, copy it, and use your quota. 

 For a local testing harness running only on your machine, it is okay to hardcode it temporarily. However, as you scale this up or move it toward production, you will absolutely need to move the OpenAI call to a backend server (like a Python FastAPI or Flask app) to keep the key hidden. 

 The Updated Code (with OpenAI Integration) 

 To make the output look like a real, print-ready document, I have added two things to the code below: 

 The OpenAI API Call: It takes the extracted_text , wraps it in a prompt asking for professional formatting, and sends it to gpt-4o . 

 Markdown Rendering: I added the marked.js library. GPT-4o will likely return the formatted text in Markdown. This library will instantly convert that Markdown into clean, styled HTML on your page so you can see exactly how it looks. 

 Here is the updated index.html : 

