Manually clicking an extension to format fifty different docker run , pip install , or git commit commands across a multi-page document is a special kind of developer torture. 

 The good news is that you don't have to keep doing this manually. You can completely automate it using Google Apps Script (the built-in JavaScript-based scripting engine inside Google Docs) or by changing your documentation pipeline. 

 Here are the best ways to tackle this, depending on how you want to handle it. 

 Option 1: The Native Way (Google Apps Script Macro) 

 You can write a short script that scans your entire document, finds text matching a specific pattern (like text wrapped in backticks `like this` or lines starting with $ ), and instantly styles them with a monospaced font, custom background color, and padding. 

 How to set it up: 

 Open your Google Doc. 

 Go to Extensions > Apps Script . 

 Delete any default code and paste the script below. 

 Click the Save icon (diskette) and then click Run . (The first time you run it, Google will ask you to grant permissions—just click Advanced > Go to Untitled project (unsafe) and allow it). 

 The Script: 

 function formatCodeBlocks() {
 var doc = DocumentApp.getActiveDocument();
 var body = doc.getBody();

 // Style definition for your code syntax
 var codeStyle = {};
 codeStyle[DocumentApp.Attribute.FONT_FAMILY] = 'Consolas';
 codeStyle[DocumentApp.Attribute.FONT_SIZE] = 10;
 codeStyle[DocumentApp.Attribute.BACKGROUND_COLOR] = '#F4F4F5'; // Sleek light gray
 codeStyle[DocumentApp.Attribute.FOREGROUND_COLOR] = '#18181B'; // Dark charcoal text

 // 1. Format Inline Code wrapped in backticks: `my-command`
 var pattern = "`[^`]+`";
 var searchResult = body.findText(pattern);

 while (searchResult !== null) {
 var element = searchResult.getElement().asText();
 var startOffset = searchResult.getStartOffset();
 var endOffset = searchResult.getEndOffset();

 // Apply styling to the text inside the backticks
 element.setAttributes(startOffset, endOffset, codeStyle);

 // Optional: Strip out the backticks after styling
 // (Uncomment the lines below if you want to remove the physical ` characters)
 // element.deleteText(endOffset, endOffset);
 // element.deleteText(startOffset, startOffset);

 searchResult = body.findText(pattern, searchResult);
 }

 // 2. Format Full Lines/Paragraphs starting with CLI prompts (e.g., "$ ", ">>> ")
 var paragraphs = body.getParagraphs();
 for (var i = 0; i < paragraphs.length; i++) {
 var text = paragraphs[i].getText();
 if (text.startsWith('$ ') || text.startsWith('>> ') || text.startsWith('uv ') || text.startsWith('python ')) {
 paragraphs[i].setAttributes(codeStyle);
 paragraphs[i].setIndentStart(18); // Give it a slight block indent
 }
 }
}

 Pro-Tip: You can assign this script to a custom drawing button in your doc or map it to a keyboard shortcut via Extensions > Macros so you can run it with a single keypress whenever you paste new text. 

 Option 2: The "Docs as Code" Pipeline (Markdown Conversion) 

 If you are writing heavy technical documentation with dozens of pages, writing directly inside a rich text editor like Google Docs is always going to feel clunky. The most scalable automation framework is to write everything in clean, raw Markdown using standard triple backticks: 

 ```bash
uv pip compile requirements.in -o requirements.txt
```

 Then, compile your Markdown files directly into beautifully formatted Google Docs using Pandoc or a quick Python utility script. 

 The Pandoc CLI approach: 

 If you have Pandoc installed, you can convert a Markdown file straight into a native .docx file with custom reference styles, then just drop it into Google Drive: 

 pandoc my_api_docs.md -o finished_doc.docx --reference-doc=my_premium_template.docx

 When uploaded to Drive, all your code blocks will inherit the exact code-block design, syntax highlights, and margins defined in your template without touching a single button manually. 

 Option 3: Use an Automatic Markdown Formatter Add-on 

 If you don't want to mess with custom scripts or terminal pipelines, you can swap out the Code Blocks extension for an add-on that handles auto-formatting on the fly . 

 Look up "Markdown to Docs" or "Docs Markdown" in the Google Workspace Marketplace. 

 These tools run in the sidebar and look for your standard text markers. Instead of highlighting text block by block, you click one button ( "Convert Document" ), and it updates every single backtick instance or indent style across all 50 pages simultaneously. 

 Are you looking to format a massive document you've already written that's currently sitting unformatted, or are you trying to optimize your setup for building new documents from scratch?

