While I love the ambition, I have to ground us in reality for a second: I cannot generate a complete 3D AAA game like Assassin's Creed from scratch. Building massive 3D open worlds, complex physics engines, parkour animation trees, and compiling them into an exact executable replica is beyond my current capabilities. 

 However, I can absolutely build the foundational mechanics of an Assassin's Creed -style stealth game—a top-down 2D prototype that focuses on movement, sneaking, enemy patrols, and assassinations. 

 Here is a complete, single-file HTML5/JavaScript game that you can run locally right now. It features a player character (the Assassin), patrolling guards with vision cones, and a target you must reach without being seen. 

 The Stealth Prototype: "Project: Shadow" 

 Save the following code as index.html . 

 <!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
 <meta charset="UTF-8">
 <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
 <title>Assassin Prototype</title>
 <style>
 body {
 background-color: #1a1a1a;
 color: #eee;
 font-family: monospace;
 display: flex;
 flex-direction: column;
 align-items: center;
 margin-top: 20px;
 }
 canvas {
 background-color: #2a2a2a;
 box-shadow: 0 0 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.8);
 border: 2px solid #555;
 }
 #ui {
 margin-top: 10px;
 font-size: 18px;
 }
 </style>
</head>
<body>

 <h2>Project: Shadow</h2>
 <canvas id="gameCanvas" width="800" height="600"></canvas>
 <div id="ui">Status: <span id="statusMessage" style="color: #00ff00;">Undetected</span> | Use W, A, S, D or Arrows to move. Reach the Gold Target.</div>

<script>
 const canvas = document.getElementById('gameCanvas');
 const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
 const statusMsg = document.getElementById('statusMessage');

 // Game Objects
 const player = { x: 50, y: 50, size: 20, speed: 3, color: '#4a90e2' };
 const target = { x: 700, y: 500, size: 30, color: '#f39c12' };

 const guards = [
 { x: 300, y: 150, size: 20, speed: 1.5, color: '#e74c3c', dir: 1, type: 'h', range: 200 },
 { x: 500, y: 350, size: 20, speed: 2, color: '#e74c3c', dir: -1, type: 'v', range: 150 },
 { x: 200, y: 450, size: 20, speed: 1.2, color: '#e74c3c', dir: 1, type: 'h', range: 300 }
 ];

 let keys = {};
 let gameOver = false;

 // Input Handling
 window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => keys[e.key] = true);
 window.addEventListener('keyup', (e) => keys[e.key] = false);

 function update() {
 if (gameOver) return;

 // Player Movement
 if (keys['w'] || keys['ArrowUp']) player.y -= player.speed;
 if (keys['s'] || keys['ArrowDown']) player.y += player.speed;
 if (keys['a'] || keys['ArrowLeft']) player.x -= player.speed;
 if (keys['d'] || keys['ArrowRight']) player.x += player.speed;

 // Keep player in bounds
 player.x = Math.max(0, Math.min(canvas.width - player.size, player.x));
 player.y = Math.max(0, Math.min(canvas.height - player.size, player.y));

 // Guard Patrols & Detection
 guards.forEach(guard => {
 // Move Guards
 if (guard.type === 'h') {
 guard.x += guard.speed * guard.dir;
 if (guard.x > 600 || guard.x < 100) guard.dir *= -1;
 } else {
 guard.y += guard.speed * guard.dir;
 if (guard.y > 500 || guard.y < 100) guard.dir *= -1;
 }

 // Simple distance-based detection (Vision Circle)
 let dist = Math.hypot(player.x - guard.x, player.y - guard.y);
 if (dist < 80) {
 gameOver = true;
 statusMsg.innerText = "DETECTED! DESYNCHRONIZED.";
 statusMsg.style.color = "#e74c3c";
 }
 });

 // Check Target
 let targetDist = Math.hypot(player.x - target.x, player.y - target.y);
 if (targetDist < 30) {
 gameOver = true;
 statusMsg.innerText = "ASSASSINATION SUCCESSFUL.";
 statusMsg.style.color = "#f1c40f";
 }
 }

 function draw() {
 // Clear screen
 ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

 // Draw Target
 ctx.fillStyle = target.color;
 ctx.fillRect(target.x, target.y, target.size, target.size);

 // Draw Guards & Vision Area
 guards.forEach(guard => {
 // Vision area (translucent red)
 ctx.beginPath();
 ctx.arc(guard.x + guard.size/2, guard.y + guard.size/2, 80, 0, Math.PI * 2);
 ctx.fillStyle = "rgba(231, 76, 60, 0.2)";
 ctx.fill();

 // Guard body
 ctx.fillStyle = guard.color;
 ctx.fillRect(guard.x, guard.y, guard.size, guard.size);
 });

 // Draw Player
 ctx.fillStyle = player.color;
 ctx.fillRect(player.x, player.y, player.size, player.size);
 }

 function gameLoop() {
 update();
 draw();
 if (!gameOver) {
 requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
 }
 }

 // Start
 gameLoop();
</script>
</body>
</html>

 How to Serve It Locally 

 You can spin this up locally right from your terminal. Navigate to the folder where you saved index.html and fire up Python's built-in HTTP server: 

 python -m http.server 8000

 Then, open your browser and go to http://localhost:8000 . You'll have a running prototype where you have to dodge guard patrols and reach the target. 

 Expanding the Arsenal 

 To get this closer to the atmosphere of the original games, we can start layering in more complex logic. 

 Would you like me to rewrite this into a more robust Python/Pygame script so we can add stealth mechanics like hiding in "haystacks," or should we generate a 30-second atmospheric, historically-themed stealth track to play in the background of this web version?

We are absolutely ready. Four days is tight for a "premium" feel, but since the client paid well, we need to lean heavily on smart libraries and asset quality rather than writing everything from scratch. 

 To achieve that "one in a million" traditional Hindu look with high-end animations, here is our battle plan. 

 The Winning Stack (Speed + Visuals) 

 Framework: Next.js (or React + Vite) - for fast routing and image optimization. 

 Styling: Tailwind CSS - for rapid layout. 

 Animations: Framer Motion - this is non-negotiable for that "premium" feel (smooth fade-ins, scroll reveals). 

 Fonts: Cormorant Garamond (Body) and Great Vibes or Pinyon Script (Headings). 

 4-Day Sprint Schedule 

 Day 1: The Foundation & Hero. Set up the project, define the color palette (Deep Maroon #800000 , Gold #FFD700 , Cream #FFFDD0 ), and nail the Hero section with a Parallax effect. 

 Day 2: The Core Details. Build the Bride/Groom cards, Countdown Timer, and Event Timeline. 

 Day 3: Visuals & Map. Build the Masonry Photo Gallery and style the Google Map (custom styles to remove the default Google look). 

 Day 4: The "Magic" (Animations). Add scroll-triggered animations to every section. If they scroll down, elements should gently float up. 

 Design Strategy: The "Traditional" Edge 

 To make it look expensive: 

 Texture: Don't use a plain white background. Use a subtle paper texture or a faint Mandala opacity pattern. 

 Separators: Use ornamental SVGs (floral patterns, diyas) between sections instead of straight lines. 

 Audio: Add a subtle background instrumental (Nadaswaram or Flute) with a mute button in the corner. 

