 What makes this Claymorphism layout so good: 

 The Box-Shadow Math: Look at --clay-outer and --clay-inner in the CSS. The var(--clay-box) variable combines drop shadows with inset white/black shadows. This gives the .workout-card and .set-bubble elements the signature puffy, inflated look. 

 The "Pressed" State: When you tap an Optional workout toggle, or hit 100% on the progress bar, you will notice the background changes to var(--clay-pressed) . Instead of looking flat, it looks like a thumb was pressed deeply into the clay. 

 Bigger Touch Targets: To support the clay look, I increased padding, made the border-radiuses larger (24px), and bumped the size of the set bubbles. It looks significantly more like a modern, native iOS application now.

This is where we go from "Web Developer" to "Product Designer." 

 Skeuomorphism (making digital UI look like physical objects) requires replacing flat colors with realistic lighting, shadows, depth, and materials. For a gym app, we want it to feel like a piece of high-end tactical hardware or gym equipment—think dark matte plastic, brushed steel, recessed LED screens, and chunky mechanical buttons. 

 Here is the complete, redesigned index.html . 

 The Skeuomorphic Upgrades: 

 Matte Hardware Background: The background now features a subtle perforated texture, mimicking heavy-duty gym flooring or equipment casing. 

 Mechanical Set Buttons: The set bubbles are now physical push-buttons. When unclicked, they are raised and cast a shadow. When clicked, they depress into the casing and light up with a realistic inner LED glow. 

 Tactical Rocker Switch: The Finisher checkbox is no longer a flat square. It is a fully styled physical sliding switch. 

 LCD Screen Timer: The floating timer has been redesigned into a recessed digital LCD panel with glowing green text set deep into a metallic bezel. 

 Engraved Branding: The "FITFORGE" logo now looks like it is engraved into a polished metal plate reflecting light. 

 Replace your entire index.html with this code. Your JavaScript logic and data structures remain 100% untouched so everything will work perfectly with your data.json . 

